---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const SITE = 'https://precisionsolutionstech.com';
const title = 'Twinkeep for iPhone & iPad Privacy Policy | Precision Solutions Tech';
const description =
  'How Twinkeep for iPhone and iPad handles your Photos library. Duplicate detection runs on your device; your photos and videos are never uploaded.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Privacy Policy', item: `${SITE}/legal/privacy/` },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 3,
        name: 'Twinkeep for iPhone & iPad',
        item: `${SITE}/legal/privacy/twinkeep-ios/`,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Twinkeep for iPhone & iPad Privacy Policy',
    url: `${SITE}/legal/privacy/twinkeep-ios/`,
    description,
    dateModified: '2026-10-11',
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Precision Solutions Tech', url: `${SITE}/` },
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/legal/privacy/twinkeep-ios/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a></li>
        <li>Twinkeep for iPhone &amp; iPad</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Twinkeep for iPhone &amp; iPad Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="meta">Last updated: October 11, 2026</p>
      <p class="lead">
        This Privacy Policy describes how <strong>Twinkeep</strong> (“we”, “us”) handles
        information when you use the Twinkeep app for iPhone and iPad (the “App”), published by
        <strong>Precision Solutions Tech</strong>, operated by <strong>SG TECHNOLOGY LLC</strong>.
        For the Mac app, see the <a href="/legal/privacy/">company privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="summary">Short version</h2>
      <p>
        Twinkeep works <strong>on your device</strong>. It reads your Photos library to find
        duplicates and does not upload your photos, videos, or anything about them to us. We
        operate no account system and no servers that receive your data. We cannot see your
        library.
      </p>

      <h2 id="local">What the App processes on your device</h2>
      <p>With your permission, the App processes on your iPhone or iPad:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Photo and video metadata</strong> — dates, sizes, dimensions, media type, and
          original file names
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Content fingerprints</strong> — hashes and similarity scores computed locally to
          detect duplicates, cached on your device so later scans are faster
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Thumbnails and previews</strong> — requested from the Photos library for display
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Clean-up records</strong> — which items you quarantined, and which smaller copies
          the App made of which originals, so you can compare and restore them
        </li>
        <li><strong>Your settings</strong> — stored in the App’s local preferences</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Widget totals</strong> — counts and sizes only (no photos, no identifiers), shared
          with the App’s own Home Screen widget on the same device
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>This processing stays on your device unless you independently share a photo or video.</p>

      <h2 id="changes-to-library">Changes to your library</h2>
      <p>
        The App only changes your Photos library when you ask it to. Items you remove are first
        added to an album named “Twinkeep Quarantine” in your library. When you delete items, iOS
        asks you to confirm, and they move to the Photos app’s Recently Deleted album.
      </p>

      <h2 id="icloud">iCloud Photos</h2>
      <p>
        If you use iCloud Photos and turn on “Check originals stored in iCloud”, iOS may download
        originals from iCloud to your device so they can be checked. That download is between your
        device and Apple; nothing passes through us. If you use iCloud Photos, changes you make —
        including deletions — sync to your other devices signed in to the same Apple Account.
      </p>

      <h2 id="permissions">Permissions the App may request, and why</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Photos</strong> — required to find duplicates and to quarantine, delete, or save smaller copies at your request</li>
        <li><strong>Notifications</strong> — only if you turn on “Notify when a scan or clean-up finishes”</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can change either in <strong>Settings → Twinkeep</strong>. Choosing Limited Access to
        Photos means Twinkeep only sees the photos you selected.
      </p>

      <h2 id="purchases">Purchases</h2>
      <p>
        Scanning is free. The one-time unlock is sold through Apple’s App Store. Apple handles
        payment; we do not receive your payment details or Apple Account information. The App checks
        your purchase with Apple on your device.
      </p>

      <h2 id="analytics">Analytics, tracking, and advertising</h2>
      <p>
        The App contains <strong>no analytics SDKs, no advertising SDKs, and no third-party
        trackers</strong>. We do not profile you, and we do not sell or share personal information.
        If that ever changes, this policy will be updated before the change ships.
      </p>

      <h2 id="network">Network access</h2>
      <p>
        Scanning, quarantine, restore, compression, and deletion do not need internet access. The
        App uses the network only through Apple’s systems: App Store purchases, and iCloud downloads
        if you turn that option on.
      </p>

      <h2 id="retention">Data retention</h2>
      <p>
        Fingerprints, scan results, and clean-up records stay on your device until you delete the
        App. The “Twinkeep Quarantine” album lives in your Photos library until you empty or delete
        it. If you contact support, we keep your message as long as needed to help you.
      </p>

      <h2 id="choices">Your rights and choices</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Choose Full Access, Limited Access, or no access to Photos at any time</li>
        <li>Restore anything from Quarantine, or forget clean-up records, in the App</li>
        <li>Delete the App to remove all of its local data</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because we do not collect your personal information, there is generally nothing for us to
        export or delete on request. If you have contacted support, you may ask us about that
        correspondence.
      </p>

      <h2 id="children">Children’s privacy</h2>
      <p>The App is not directed to children under 13 (or the minimum age in your jurisdiction).</p>

      <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
      <p>We may update this policy. The “Last updated” date will change when we do.</p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Privacy questions:
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/ios-apps/twinkeep/">Twinkeep for iPhone &amp; iPad</a>
        ·
        <a href="/ios-apps/twinkeep/support/">Support</a>
        ·
        <a href="/legal/privacy/">Company privacy policy</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
